> [!WARNING]
> ## Open Question: Supabase RLS Violation
> The Supabase schema you ran includes the following Row Level Security (RLS) policy:
> ```sql
> CREATE POLICY "Users: own data only" ON users
>   FOR ALL USING (clerk_id = current_setting('app.clerk_id', true));
> ```
> This policy relies on a custom Postgres setting (`app.clerk_id`). Since we are currently using the `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`, any attempt to insert a user from our backend will fail with an **RLS violation** because this setting is null.
> 
> **How would you like to proceed?**
> 1. **(Recommended)** Provide your **Supabase Service Role Key** (found in Supabase Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API). We can store it as `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` in `.env.local`. This allows our secure backend Server Actions to bypass RLS and create the user.
> 2. Disable RLS on the `users` table temporarily.
> 3. Add an RPC function in Supabase to set the `app.clerk_id` setting.

Please let me know how you'd like to handle the Supabase RLS issue, and if you approve this implementation plan!
